    "content": "Somali Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre issued a directive on Wednesday ordering all government ministers and other officials to fully implement a competency-based system for hiring and appointing civil servants. The order, which takes effect immediately, is based on Article 161(1) of the Somali Federal Constitution, Article 7 of the Civil Servants Law, and other relevant regulations.\n\nThe directive emphasizes that all recruitment and appointments must be based on merit and priority, with no exceptions or delays. It applies to all federal government institutions, including ministries, agencies, and other public bodies. The Prime Minister also instructed the Civil Service Commission to oversee the implementation and ensure compliance.\n\nThis move is part of broader efforts by the Somali federal government to reform public administration, improve service delivery, and combat corruption. In recent years, the government has faced criticism for appointments perceived as favoring clan affiliations or personal connections over qualifications. The new directive aims to address these concerns by institutionalizing a transparent and fair hiring process.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development holds particular significance. Many diaspora professionals have long expressed interest in contributing to Somalia's reconstruction but have been deterred by opaque recruitment practices. A merit-based system could open doors for skilled individuals to return and serve in key positions, leveraging their expertise gained abroad. It also signals a commitment to good governance that may encourage increased diaspora investment and engagement.\n\nThe Prime Minister's directive is expected to face challenges in implementation, given entrenched interests and logistical constraints. However, it represents a clear policy shift toward professionalism in the civil service. Observers note that successful execution will require strong oversight and political will.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/17/xukuumadda-soomaaliya-%f0%9d%90%a8%f0%9d%90%a8-%f0%9d%90%a4%f0%9d%90%ae-%f0%9d%90%9d%f0%9d%90%a1%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%b0%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%aad%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%b2-%f0%9d%90%a1/",
