    "title": "Solar Irrigation Boosts Yields and Slashes Costs for Afgoye Farmers",
    "original_title": "Solar irrigation doubles production and cuts costs for Afgoye farmers",
    "slug": "solar-irrigation-boosts-yields-and-slashes-costs-for-afgoye-farmers",
    "summary": "Farmers in Jarran village, Afgoye district, have doubled agricultural production and reduced irrigation expenses after switching from fuel-powered pumps to solar systems.",
    "content": "Farmers in Jarran village, located in the Lower Shabelle region's Afgoye district, have reported a significant increase in agricultural output and a sharp decrease in irrigation costs after adopting solar-powered water pumps. The shift comes as fuel prices continue to rise, making traditional irrigation methods increasingly unaffordable for local growers.\n\nMohamed Nur Isaq, who cultivates a four-hectare farm, told Radio Ergo that his production has more than doubled since he installed a solar system in January. Previously, he spent between $100 and $150 for each irrigation cycle using fuel-powered pumps. With solar energy, those recurring expenses have been virtually eliminated, allowing him to reinvest in his farm and increase his harvest.\n\nOther farmers in the village have experienced similar benefits. The adoption of solar technology has not only cut costs but also reduced reliance on volatile fuel markets, providing a more stable and predictable farming operation. The initiative is seen as a model for sustainable agriculture in a region where access to reliable energy has long been a challenge.\n\nAfgoye district is part of Somalia's most fertile agricultural areas, producing crops such as maize, sorghum, and sesame. However, years of conflict, drought, and economic instability have hampered productivity. The move to solar irrigation represents a grassroots response to these challenges, leveraging renewable energy to improve food security and livelihoods.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development highlights the potential of investing in solar technology back home. Many diaspora members support family farms or run agricultural businesses in the region. The success in Jarran village demonstrates how clean energy can enhance productivity and reduce operational costs, making remittances and investments go further. It also aligns with broader efforts to promote climate-resilient farming practices in Somalia.\n\nAs fuel prices remain high and climate change continues to affect rainfall patterns, solar irrigation could play a crucial role in sustaining Somalia's agricultural sector. The experience of Afgoye's farmers offers a scalable solution that could be replicated in other parts of the country, potentially transforming rural economies and reducing dependence on costly fossil fuels.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/solar-irrigation-doubles-production-and-cuts-costs-for-afgoye-farmers/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
    "source_name": "Radio Ergo",
